> Gee, how cheerful and optimistic.  In another section he compares
> technological man to an alcoholic on a keg of wine.  He argues that
> the technological man simply doesn't have the will power to place
> humanity and nature before gung-ho expansion of technology for profit.
> I'm not at all convinced that he is right.

We can't seem to get the industrialists to stop destroying the rainforest, which 
most agree is necessary for the health of our planet. That seems to indicate 
that he is right.

  If you take this argument,
> it essentially argues that there can somehow be a group of people who
> "know" the dangers, and if only we follow them we will all be better
> off.
> 
> Gee, I'm sure plenty of people from the Soviet Union and China could
> tell us in detail what this can lead to.  Arguments like the
> Unabomer's fail to understand a common problem with human beings: they
> rarely realize how limited their understanding of the world is.  You
> cannot possibly have a bunch of people who can effectively shape our
> destinies and make everyone happy -- it simply doesn't work.  Along
> similar lines, you can't have a "war council" which decides what "all
> the right moves" will be in attacking technology, they are bound to
> make a horrible mistake.  You take this, and add to it the fact that
> they _expect_ problems and chaos, indeed that they view it as
> necessary, and I believe you are bound to end up with a huge amount of
> unnecessary suffering.
> 
> I also believe that any high-revolutionaries are bound to make a
> power-play like what happened after the Russian Revolution, all for
> the good of the people, of course.  Yeah, right.

I can't argue with you on this. The Unabomber isn't exactly playing with a full 
deck. (meredith translation: he's a nut.)
Still, I do think he's right that our dependence on high-tech is dangerous and 
unhealthy... medically, sociologically, and in an evolutionary sense. In other 
words, the Unabomber correctly identified the problem. He just got a little 
carried away with his solution. 
A phased downsizing of our overly huge techno-world would be better (i.e. small 
villages, solar power, etc.) But it ain't gonna happen. Dream on! The Unabomber 
can dream of his revolution and I'll dream of my eco-communitarian future, and 
it's all dreams, neither of them's gonna happen. Humanity may reform, but it'll 
probably take a big crash first. Oh yeah, that's in the Manifesto too.

"If the system breaks down, the consequences will still be very painful. But the 
bigger the system grows, the more disastrous the results of its breakdown will 
be. So if it is to break down, it had best break down sooner than later."
Which is like saying, a junkie had better kick the habit before the heroin kills 
him, and it is easier to kick a 1-bag-a-day habit than a 10-bag-a-day habit.
This warning is valid. It's just a shame he thought bombs would make a 
difference.

--Shadow





